// @error: array element has type string, but the array is declared [i64; 2]
//
// Both array-literal paths called `checkExprWithHint(elem, hint.element)` and threw the
// answer away, so nothing ever compared them. `var x: [i64; 2] = ["a", "b"]` type-checked,
// and the mismatch surfaced as an LLVM error — `'%t.3' defined with type '%String' but
// expected 'i64'` — which is a type error escaping to clang and reported in a language the
// user does not write. `Vec<i64> = ["a"]` had the identical shape.
//
// Found by tests/typeAnnotationFidelity.test.ts, which asks whether a value of the WRONG
// shape can satisfy an annotation.
fn main() {
    let x: [i64; 2] = ["a", "b"]
    print(x[0])
}
